<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Welf I, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1035–1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Welf IV · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">House of Este</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Welf I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, counted as Welf IV of his family, was the founder of the Bavarian line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a>. Made duke by Emperor Henry IV in 1070, he was deposed in 1077 for joining the emperor's opponents in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a> and restored in 1096. With his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judith-of-flanders">Judith of Flanders</a> he endowed Weingarten Abbey. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 1101 on his way home from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/welf-i-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Welf I, Duke of Bavaria" width="320" height="530" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wgt_Stifterb%C3%BCchlein_25v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Welf was the son of Albert Azzo II, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and Kunigunde of Altdorf. When his maternal uncle Welf, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, died childless in 1055, he inherited the uncle's property. In 1062 he married Ethelinde of Northeim, daughter of Otto, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. His father remained firm in the interests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>, but Welf's marriage and his German inheritance made him more concerned with German affairs, and he supported Emperor Henry IV. He was the first member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">House of Este</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>When Duke Otto became an enemy of Henry IV and lost his duchy, Welf stayed loyal to the emperor, repudiated and divorced Ethelinde at Henry's command and in 1070 was appointed Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> at Goslar in Otto's place. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> accepted him quietly. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a> he changed sides, supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a> and the election of Rudolf of Rheinfelden in March 1077. Henry declared him deposed, but with the support of his Bavarians and his father in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> he kept his place and openly defied the ban in May 1077.</p><p>After Gregory died in 1085 Welf, though still on the side of the church, relaxed his efforts for Rudolf. After his divorce he had married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judith-of-flanders">Judith of Flanders</a>, daughter of Baldwin IV, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, and widow of Tostig Godwinson. In 1089 his son Welf married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Tuscany</a>, which strengthened ties with the pope, but when the younger Welf divorced her in 1095 the father made amends with Henry IV and was reconfirmed as duke.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1099 Welf joined the expedition later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of 1101, with William IX of Aquitaine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-vermandois">Hugh of Vermandois</a> and Ida of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He prevented a clash between crusaders who had been pillaging Byzantine territory and the Pecheneg mercenaries of the Byzantine emperor. After passing Heraclea in September his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> were ambushed and massacred by the Turkish troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-i">Kilij Arslan I</a>, the Seljuq Sultan of Rûm. Welf himself escaped and died on the way back at Paphos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> on 6 November 1101. He was buried at Weingarten Abbey and succeeded as duke by his son Welf II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Welf I of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1094 Judith of Flanders, wife of <b>Welf IV</b>, gave it a relic of the Holy Blood.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-weingarten">Imperial Abbey of Weingarten</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Paphos, in what is now <a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.78,32.43&amp;year=1077" aria-label="Paphos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Welf I, Duke of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5170 4590h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5170 4590h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.78,32.43&amp;year=1077">What was here in 1077? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
